        <mxCell id="LHn__QqQNdfq77utMTcS-39" parent="1" style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffe6cc;strokeColor=#d79b00;" value="&lt;h3&gt;Recommended Best Practice&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;ol start=&quot;1&quot;&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Phase 1 (Validation):&lt;/b&gt; Pick a known IP range containing 10–30 target devices (e.g., Linux or Windows servers). Define an explicit range (&lt;code&gt;10.x.x.10&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;10.x.x.40&lt;/code&gt;). Verify credentials, CI classification, and CMDB identification rules.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Phase 2 (Exclusions):&lt;/b&gt; Use Discovery &lt;b&gt;Exclusion Ranges&lt;/b&gt; to omit DHCP pools (user laptops/phones), network appliances you manage elsewhere, or security honeypots.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Phase 3 (Scale):&lt;/b&gt; Only after Discovery schedules, MID Servers, and CMDB health rules are tuned should you expand to broader ranges or larger subnets.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="210" width="560" x="790" y="4105" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
